          <a href="#if-you-coat-over" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. If You Coat Over Anyway</a>
          <a href="#special-cases" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Specific Situations</a>
          <a href="#why-paint-fails" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Why Floor Paint Fails</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Epoxy is designed to key mechanically into open concrete pores. Painted concrete has no open pores — it has a film of paint. Coat over it and you have not bonded epoxy to your slab; you have bonded epoxy to a layer of paint, and the whole floor now depends on how well that paint was applied by someone else, years ago.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: You Can, But You Probably Should Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy will physically stick to most sound paint, and there are situations where coating over it works out. But it changes what you are relying on. In a properly prepared installation, the coating is anchored into the profiled surface of the concrete itself, and the bond is as strong as the slab. Over paint, the strongest possible bond is the strength of the paint’s grip on the concrete underneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because garage floor paint is typically the weakest coating in common use, with a service life of one to three years, and because it was often applied with minimal preparation in the first place. Building a fifteen-year system on top of a two-year one is a poor structural decision even when it looks fine on day one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Failure You Are Risking</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">It does not fail at the epoxy. It fails at the paint.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When this goes wrong, the epoxy and the paint lift off the concrete together as a single sheet, usually starting where a tyre sits. You then have a removal job that is substantially harder than the one you avoided, because you are stripping two bonded layers instead of one.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The recommended path in almost every case is removal: grind the paint off, expose and profile the concrete, and coat a properly prepared slab. That is what every epoxy manufacturer’s data sheet asks for, and it is the difference between a floor that lasts a few years and one that lasts a decade or more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Test Whether the Paint Will Hold
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are seriously considering coating over rather than removing, test first. These take minutes and they are far cheaper than discovering the answer after the epoxy is down.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">The tape test</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Score a small crosshatch through the paint with a utility knife, press strong tape firmly over it, and pull sharply. If paint lifts off with the tape, the bond is already failing and nothing should be coated over it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">The scrape test</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Work a putty knife under an edge or at a chip. Sound paint resists and comes away only in small flakes. Paint that peels back in sheets is not a foundation for anything.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">The water test</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sprinkle water in several places. If it beads and sits, the surface is sealed and non-porous, which tells you nothing will penetrate. If it soaks in somewhere, the paint has worn through there and the floor is a patchwork of two different substrates.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Check for hot tire damage</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Look where the cars park. Lifted, softened or tread-marked paint in the tyre positions means the paint cannot survive that heat, and adding epoxy on top does not change what is underneath.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Identify the paint if you can</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Latex or acrylic floor paint is a poor base for epoxy. An old epoxy or urethane coating in sound condition is a considerably better one. If you have no idea what it is, treat that as an argument for removal.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The honest reading of these tests: passing them all means coating over is a defensible compromise. Failing any one of them means removal, because a single failing area will spread underneath the new coating and take the surrounding floor with it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="removal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Remove the Paint Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the recommended route, and it is more accessible than most people expect because the equipment can be rented by the day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Diamond Grinding</h3>

          <p>
            A walk-behind concrete grinder with diamond tooling removes coatings and simultaneously profiles the concrete, which is exactly the two-in-one outcome you want. Connected to a dust extractor it is a manageable, if noisy, day’s work for a single garage. This is what professional installers use and it produces the best possible substrate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shot Blasting</h3>

          <p>
            A shot blaster propels steel media at the floor and recovers it, stripping the coating and leaving an aggressive profile. It is fast and clean on large areas, less commonly rented for a single residential garage, and it can be over-aggressive on soft or damaged concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chemical Stripping</h3>

          <p>
            Strippers soften the paint so it can be scraped away. This is the option that needs no heavy equipment, but it is slow, messy, generates waste that needs proper disposal, and — critically — it does not profile the concrete. After stripping you still have to grind or at minimum aggressively etch, so it adds a step rather than replacing one. Reserve it for edges and corners the grinder cannot reach.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Rent a grinder with a dust shroud and connect a proper extractor; concrete dust is a genuine respiratory hazard.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Wear eye protection, a P100 respirator and hearing protection.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Work edges and corners with a hand grinder or an edging attachment.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Expect the floor to look mottled afterwards — you are aiming for profile, not appearance.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Vacuum thoroughly, then vacuum again; dust left behind is a bond breaker.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="if-you-coat-over" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. If You Decide to Coat Over Anyway
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are legitimate reasons to accept the compromise — a rental property, a floor you expect to redo in a few years, a budget that does not stretch to grinding. If that is the decision, these steps give it the best chance.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Remove everything loose</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Scrape off every flake, blister and peeling area. Anything that lifts willingly must come off, or the new coating will lift with it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Feather the edges of what remains</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sand the boundaries between remaining paint and bare concrete so they taper. A hard edge telegraphs through the finished epoxy as a visible line.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Abrade the entire painted surface</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sand or lightly grind the whole floor to dull the paint and create mechanical tooth. Epoxy will not grip a smooth, glossy paint film.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Degrease thoroughly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Clean the whole floor and treat oil stains specifically. Contamination under the new coating causes localised failure regardless of everything else.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Vacuum and tack off all dust</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sanding dust is a bond breaker. Vacuum, then wipe with a damp cloth and let it dry completely.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Test a small area first</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Coat a square foot, let it cure fully, then try to lift it with a scraper. This is the only real proof before committing the whole floor.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Use an epoxy primer designed for coated substrates</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some manufacturers offer primers formulated to bond to existing coatings. Check the technical data sheet rather than assuming a standard product will work.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Set Your Expectations Accordingly</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Assume the shorter lifespan and plan for it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A well-executed epoxy over sound paint can look excellent and serve for a few years. What it will not do is deliver the eight to twelve years of a high-solids system on prepared concrete, because the paint underneath sets the ceiling. Budget for redoing it sooner rather than being surprised.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="special-cases" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Specific Situations That Change the Answer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over an Existing Epoxy Floor</h3>

          <p>
            This is a genuinely different and far more favourable case. Epoxy bonds well to epoxy provided the existing coating is sound and properly abraded. Scuff-sand the whole floor to dull the gloss, clean it thoroughly, and recoat. If the existing epoxy is peeling or blistering, however, the same rule applies — the failure is at its bond line and nothing on top will fix that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over Concrete Sealer</h3>

          <p>
            Poor. A penetrating sealer has deliberately filled the pore structure that epoxy needs to key into, which is the opposite of what you want. Film-forming sealers present the same problem as paint. Sealed concrete needs grinding, and a water test that beads rather than soaks is your indication that a sealer is present.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over Partially Worn Paint</h3>

          <p>
            The most common real-world situation and one of the trickiest. A floor where paint remains in the corners but traffic has worn through to bare concrete in the middle is two substrates in one room, and epoxy behaves differently on each — different absorption, different sheen, different bond strength. This patchwork is a strong argument for removing everything and starting from one consistent surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over Paint With Any Moisture Sign</h3>

          <p>
            If the existing paint shows blistering, bubbling or white powdery deposits, the slab is likely transmitting moisture vapour. Adding epoxy makes that worse by adding a second impermeable layer. Test with a taped plastic sheet overnight and address the moisture before considering any coating.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-paint-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Garage Floor Paint Fails in the First Place
          </h2>

          <p>
            Understanding why the existing paint is in the condition it is in tells you a great deal about whether anything should go on top of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Garage floor paint — typically a latex or acrylic product, sometimes an alkyd — forms a thin film that sits on the concrete rather than keying into it. It was usually applied with minimal preparation, often nothing more than sweeping and perhaps an acid etch, because that is what the product instructions call for. The film is thin, comparatively soft, and has modest adhesion by design.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Failure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Means for Recoating</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling in sheets</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor adhesion from the start</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove entirely; nothing can bond over it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tyre tread marks lifted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hot tire pickup on a soft film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove; the same will happen through epoxy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worn through in traffic paths</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin film, normal abrasion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two substrates now; remove all of it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Blistering or white deposits</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture vapour from the slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stop; address moisture before any coating</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dulled but intact everywhere</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal surface wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The one case where coating over may be viable</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Only that last row is a reasonable candidate for coating over, and even then it is a compromise rather than a recommendation. Every other row describes a floor where the existing paint is telling you plainly that it cannot support another layer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Epoxy Will Stick to Anything</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy bonds mechanically into open concrete pores, and a painted floor has none — it has a film. Coating over paint means the entire system depends on that paint’s grip on the slab, which for typical garage floor paint is the weakest link in the assembly. Test before you assume it will hold.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Paint That Fails the Tape Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a crosshatch and a strip of tape lifts paint off the concrete, the bond has already failed and adding epoxy simply attaches a stronger, heavier layer to something that is letting go. The result is both layers coming off together, which is a harder removal job than the one you were avoiding.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chemically Stripping and Then Coating Immediately</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stripper removes the paint but leaves the concrete unprofiled and often with residue in the pores. Epoxy applied to a stripped-but-unground slab bonds poorly. Stripping is a step before grinding, not an alternative to it — reserve it for edges and corners the grinder cannot reach.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating a Floor That Is Half Paint and Half Bare Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The two substrates absorb differently, cure differently and bond with very different strength, so the finished floor varies in sheen and fails first along the boundaries. If traffic has worn through the paint in the middle of the garage, remove all of it and coat one consistent surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Blisters in the Existing Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bubbling or white powdery deposits in old floor paint usually indicate moisture vapour rising through the slab. Adding an epoxy layer on top traps it further and guarantees blistering. Test with a taped plastic sheet overnight and deal with the moisture source before any coating goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you epoxy over a painted garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Physically yes, but it is not recommended. Epoxy bonds mechanically into open concrete pores, and paint covers them, so the whole system relies on the paint’s grip on the slab. Since garage floor paint typically lasts one to three years, you are building a long-lived coating on a short-lived foundation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if I can epoxy over my existing paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Test it. Score a crosshatch, press tape firmly over it and pull — if paint lifts, do not coat over it. Try to work a putty knife under an edge; sound paint resists. Sprinkle water to see whether the surface is sealed or worn through in places. Failing any of these means removal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to remove all the old paint before applying epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For the best result, yes. Diamond grinding removes the paint and profiles the concrete in one operation, giving epoxy the open surface it is designed to key into. If you coat over paint instead, remove everything loose, feather the edges, abrade the whole surface and accept a shorter service life.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best way to remove paint from a garage floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Diamond grinding with a walk-behind grinder and dust extraction, which strips the coating and profiles the slab simultaneously. Shot blasting is the professional alternative on large areas. Chemical stripping needs no equipment but is slow, messy and does not profile the concrete, so grinding is still required afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put epoxy over an existing epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and this is a much better case than painting over paint. Epoxy bonds well to epoxy provided the existing coating is sound. Scuff-sand the whole floor to dull the gloss, clean thoroughly and recoat. If the existing epoxy is peeling or blistering, it must come off first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will epoxy over paint last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less than epoxy over properly prepared concrete, because the paint underneath sets the ceiling. A high-solids epoxy on a ground slab can give eight to twelve years; over sound paint you should plan for considerably less and budget to redo it sooner. If the paint fails, both layers lift together.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

