        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            You can put epoxy over a painted garage floor, and it will stick on day one. What you cannot do is make the finished floor stronger than the paint underneath it, and garage floor paint is typically the weakest coating in common use. When it lets go, both layers come off together and you are left with a harder removal job than the one you skipped.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Test before deciding. A crosshatch and a strip of tape, a putty knife under an edge, and a sprinkle of water tell you most of what you need to know in five minutes. Pass all three and coating over is a defensible compromise for a rental or a short-horizon floor. Fail any one and the answer is removal.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The recommended route is to grind the paint off and profile the slab in the same operation, which is a rentable day’s work and gives epoxy the open concrete it was formulated to bond into. That single decision is worth more to the floor’s lifespan than any difference between the products you are choosing between.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
